The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
gf_list_del(s->objects);
while (gf_list_count(s->route_sessions)) {
    GF_ROUTESession *rsess = gf_list_pop_back(s->route_sessions);
    gf_route_route_session_del(rsess);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the objects in `s->objects` are deleted after the route sessions in the `gf_route_service_del` function. This improper order means that if any route session tries to access `s->objects` after it has been deleted, it will lead to dereferencing freed memory, potentially causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
while (gf_list_count(s->objects)) {
    GF_LCTObject *o = gf_list_pop_back(s->objects);
    gf_route_lct_obj_del(o);
}
gf_list_del(s->objects);
```
Reason:
The fix involves moving the deletion of `s->objects` before the deletion of `s->route_sessions`. This ensures that all objects are properly deleted before any route sessions are processed, preventing any access to freed memory and thus avoiding the potential crash.

